{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.b5b04e2255","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-88","passage":"<p>The owl, a species that hunts mainly at ______ in hollow trees.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["dusk nests","dusk, nests","dusk—nests","dusk; nests"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The phrase “a species that hunts mainly at dusk” is extra information about “the owl.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.e69892af6c","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-11","passage":"<p>The discovery came from an unexpected ______ mold growing on a forgotten dish.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["source; a","source, and a","source a","source: a"],"answer":"D","explanation":"“The discovery came from an unexpected source” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.b4b5ddec75","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-16","passage":"<p>The hikers packed layers for every kind of ______, wind, and sudden cold.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["weather rain","weather, rain","weather; rain","weather: rain"],"answer":"D","explanation":"“The hikers packed layers for every kind of weather” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.7aa2796ac5","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-21","passage":"<p>The town's economy relies on two ______ and tourism.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["industries fishing","industries, and fishing","industries; fishing","industries: fishing"],"answer":"D","explanation":"“The town's economy relies on two industries” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.1de40e85dd","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-33","passage":"<p>The team's success came down to one ______ relentless defense.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["factor its","factor; its","factor: its","factor, and its"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The team's success came down to one factor” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."}
